course: dictionary
lesson: 7546
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. You can find the right one by reading the words around it, then asking which meaning fits best.
explain: Take prefect. In British and Commonwealth schools, a prefect is an older pupil chosen to help keep order and help younger pupils. Prefects may wear badges, help at assemblies and watch the corridors. In school stories they can be helpful or bossy, and the story's clues show which.
explain: The sentence «a new school rule that suits the prefects and burdens everyone else» has a clue: school rules. So here prefects are the people who keep order. The same word can also name an official in France or an officer in ancient Rome, so always check the clues before you choose.
fact: A prefect is an older pupil chosen to help keep order and help younger pupils at school.
fact: Phrases you may see are «head prefect» and «be made a prefect».
fact: The head boy or head girl leads the prefects.
fact: In France, a prefect is an official who runs a region.
fact: In ancient Rome, a prefect was an important officer.
exercise: Maya is made a prefect at her school. What is a prefect? | A younger pupil who sits at the front | A school bus driver | *An older pupil chosen to help keep order and help younger pupils | A teacher who marks the maths tests
exercise: In a school story, the prefects watch the corridors and wear badges. Where do they watch? | In the kitchen | In the library | On the football pitch | *In the corridors
exercise: A French story says, «The prefect runs the whole region.» What does prefect mean here? | *An official who runs a region | A school bell | A kind of bread | An older pupil
test: Ella reads «The prefect checked the corridor.» What is the best meaning here? | *An older pupil who helps keep order at school | A running race | A cake | A dinner plate
test: What should you do when a word has more than one meaning? | Skip the sentence and never come back | *Read the words around it and check which meaning fits | Look only at the picture | Always pick the first meaning you know
exam: In ancient Rome, what was a prefect? | A bell ringer | A cook in a palace | *An important officer | A pupil who was bossy
